<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiang Guangnai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiang Guangnai was a Chinese general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, commander of the Nineteenth Route Army, which resisted the Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in January–March 1932. Transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-tingkai">Cai Tingkai</a> led the Fujian Rebellion of November 1933 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, which was crushed early in 1934. He later joined the Revolutionary Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> as minister of the textile industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiang-guangnai.jpg" alt="Jiang Guangnai" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jiang Guangnai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiang_Guangnai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jiang Guangnai was born on 17 December 1888 in Dongguan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. He served as a bodyguard to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, and in 1932 he was promoted to general and made commander in chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/19th-route-army">19th Route Army</a>, which he led in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> during the January 28 incident.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the ceasefire at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/19th-route-army">19th Route Army</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> to put down the Communist insurgency there. The army won some battles against the Communists and then made peace with them. On 22 November 1933 Jiang joined the rising that set up the People's Revolutionary Government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, outside the control of Chiang's Nanjing government. It found no backing among the other warlords or among all the Communists, and when it fell Jiang escaped with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>; the rest of the army was disbanded and its men spread among other units of the National Revolutionary Army.</p><p>Jiang returned to command during the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. From 1939 to 1944 he was deputy commander in chief of the 4th War Area, and in 1945 of the 7th War Area. After the Communist victory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> made him minister of textiles of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, a post he held from 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, when most senior officials were set upon by the Red Guards, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> protected Jiang. A day after Red Guards had stormed his home, Zhou had him stand almost beside Mao on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> to review the Red Guards, and walked over in front of them to ask how he was after the visit. Jiang answered that the Red Guards had been fairly civilised, and they left him alone afterwards. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 8 June 1967. His residence in Dongguan is now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiang Guangnai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its commanders Cai Tingkai and <b>Jiang Guangnai</b>, with the Nationalist dissidents Li Jishen and Chen Mingshu, broke with Chiang Kai-shek and in November 1933 proclaimed the People's Revolutionary Government of the Republic of China at Fuzhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/fujian-peoples-government">Fujian People's Government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dongguan, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.05,113.75&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Dongguan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jiang Guangnai"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4509 5580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4509 5580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.05,113.75&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
